Verruca Plantaris
Common Infections and Afflictions
Hyperkeratotic sole papule, Disrupted skin lines, Thrombosed capillaries upon paring

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-046
Reason for Visit: Severe foot pain and inability to walk due to a painful "corn."
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting acute, severe discomfort during weight-bearing activities, effectively rendering them immobile within the home. The patient describes a "sharp, stabbing" pain on the sole of the foot, which has intensified over the past 24 hours. Given the functional immobility and the need to differentiate this from an infected foreign body, deep tissue abscess, or diabetic ulceration, an urgent home assessment is required to perform a physical evaluation and provide immediate localized treatment. Delaying this assessment poses a risk of secondary infection and ongoing functional distress, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: Every time I step down, it feels like I'm walking on a sharp needle. I can't even get to the kitchen.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have developed gradually but reached a threshold of functional impairment in the last 24 hours. The patient reports a firm and hyperkeratotic papule on the sole of the foot. Notes a slowly enlarging rough surface and significant discomfort during weight-bearing. Pain is sharply elicited by lateral pressure (pinching the lesion) rather than direct vertical pressure. Admits to regular communal shower use at a public pool. Denies fever, redness spreading up the foot, or history of diabetes.
Functional Status: Currently restricted to hopping or using furniture for support due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Fall risk due to antalgic gait.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 78 bpm | Temp: 36.6 °C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Hopping on the unaffected foot to reach the exam chair.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Clinical diagnosis based on paring of the lesion.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the right plantar surface reveals a 6mm firm, hyperkeratotic papule over the second metatarsal head. There is clear disruption of the normal dermatoglyphic skin lines. Upon gentle paring of the overlying callus, pinpoint black dots (thrombosed capillaries) are clearly visualized, confirming the diagnosis. Significant pain is elicited by lateral pressure (pinching). No significant localized erythema, fluctuance, or discharge present. Neurovascular status of the foot is intact.
Environmental Observations: Residence is safe; patient has moved essential items to a single floor.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient attempted to use a "corn plaster" with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Verruca Plantaris - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Plantar Corn (Clavus) - Ruled out by the disruption of skin lines and the presence of pinpoint bleeding upon paring, whereas corns maintain skin lines and lack thrombosed capillaries.
Foreign Body Granuloma - Unlikely given the classic appearance and lack of a specific penetrating trauma history, though paring helped exclude an embedded object.
Plantar Malignancy (e.g., Amelanotic Melanoma) - Unlikely given the classic viral features, but persistent or atypical lesions warrant follow-up.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient’s acute functional immobility necessitated an urgent evaluation to rule out deep-seated infection or foreign body and to provide immediate mechanical relief. Direct physical examination and paring confirmed a plantar wart (verruca). By debulking the hyperkeratotic tissue and providing specialized padding in the home, the patient’s ability to ambulate safely was immediately restored, preventing a likely fall or a visit to the emergency department for "sudden foot pain." This intervention definitively could not wait until standard business hours, fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed paring of the hyperkeratotic callus to reduce vertical pressure pain. Prescribed a topical salicylic acid preparation to be applied strictly to the lesion daily. Applied a "donut" shaped felt pad to redistribute weight away from the lesion.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically prevent the virus from spreading or recurring, strictly wear flip-flops in all communal areas, including your own bathroom if shared. This physical barrier stops the virus from shedding onto surfaces where it can survive and reinfect you or others.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Provided felt padding for pressure redistribution.
Follow-up: Review with a Podiatrist in 1-2 weeks for formal cryotherapy or ongoing debridement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek medical review if the area becomes red, hot, swollen, and begins to leak pus, or if the pain becomes worse despite the padding.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; warts are caused by a virus and can be stubborn to treat.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain will be improved immediately by the paring and padding. The wart itself may take several weeks or months of daily acid treatment to completely resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if you develop red streaks traveling from the lesion up your foot.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: A virus has hijacked the skin cells on your foot, causing them to grow into a hard, painful lump with its own blood supply (the black dots). The paring I did today removed the "hard cap," and the acid you will use daily will slowly burn away the infected cells.
Actionable Steps: Before applying the acid each night, gently use a pumice stone or emery board to file away the dead white skin on top; this allows the medication to reach the active virus underneath. Do not share shoes, socks, or towels with anyone else.

